{
  "example": "A requirements input selects `requirements.cli.*`. A selected requirement references `glossary.command`, which is captured as a separate definition input despite not matching that selection. An unreferenced definition named `requirements.cli.unrelated` is not selected merely because its name matches the selection. {cite}`requirements.definitions.selection`",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: false.** The implementation is correct, and the test covers most of the scenario. It never asserts the \"no separate input for the unrelated definition\" outcome, which is the point of this example.\n\n**What the test covers.** `tests/definitions.rs::globs_exclude_terms_but_capture_referenced_terms_outside_the_glob` (lines 127-146) is the only `{test}` reference to `requirements.definitions.glob_dependency`.\n- The `fixture()` and `capture()` helpers set up the `rules.*` glob input on `rules.ready`.\n- `rules.ready` references `{term}glossary.a`, which sits outside the `rules.*` glob. `glossary.a` and `glossary.b` reference each other.\n- The test appends an unreferenced `rules.unrelated` definition. Its name matches the `rules.*` glob.\n- It asserts `inputs[0].matches == [\"rules.ready\"]`, so the glob input does not select the unrelated definition.\n- It asserts `inputs[1].definition == \"glossary.a\"` and `inputs[2].definition == \"glossary.b\"`. This shows that referenced definitions outside the glob are captured as separate definition inputs.\n\n**Why the implementation behaves as the example says.**\n- `InputSelector::resolve` (`src/inputs.rs`, `Self::Requirements` arm) skips `DefinitionKind::Term`, so only requirements and examples are matched.\n- `terms::dependencies` (`src/terms.rs`) derives dependencies only from `{term}` references. It does so for the subject text, the text of selected requirement bodies, and the transitive closure of definition bodies. Name-prefix matches never contribute.\n\n**Gap in the test.** The second half of the example and of `requirements.definitions.selection` is \"sharing a selection's matching name prefix alone MUST NOT make a definition a dependency\". The test does not guard that.\n- It never asserts the number of inputs. The sibling test asserts `len() == 3` for the base fixture, but this test does not.\n- It checks only indices 0 to 2. A regression that also captured the unreferenced `rules.unrelated` as a `definition` input would add an input at index 3, and the test would still pass.\n- `validate_dependencies` only checks that each required definition appears exactly once. It does not reject extra definition inputs, so no other check would catch the regression.\n- The `status_markdown(...).contains(\"rules.unrelated\")` assertion is vacuous. `status_markdown_with_options` (`src/commands.rs`) skips every `DefinitionKind::Term`, so `rules.unrelated` never appears in the status output whether or not it was wrongly selected or captured.\n\n**What would close the gap.** Assert that no input has `definition == \"rules.unrelated\"`, or assert `inputs.len() == 3`. The test has no `FIXME` or other acknowledged gap, so the result is `false` rather than `FIXME`.",
  "inputs": [
